WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.620
Welcome to section eight, pulmonary rehabilitation.

2
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:11.246
Rehab does not significantly improve the PFTs; it changes what the patient can do.

3
00:00:11.246 --> 00:00:33.868
The RT's three tasks on the exam are: keep exercise safe by holding oxygenation above a specific threshold, teach breathing retraining including diaphragmatic and pursed-lip, and know the difference between a shallow chest pattern, true hyperventilation, and true hypoventilation.

4
00:00:33.868 --> 00:00:38.150
We will finish with the exercise prescription itself.

5
00:00:39.567 --> 00:00:59.616
Hess chapter twenty-four says all exercise training should be performed under conditions of adequate arterial oxygenation, defined as PaO2 greater than fifty-five millimeters of mercury and SpO2 greater than eighty-eight percent.

6
00:00:59.616 --> 00:01:03.905
Numbers to memorize: eighty-eight and fifty-five.

7
00:01:03.905 --> 00:01:14.499
Hess writes the limit with a greater-than sign; the RCP 203 deck accepts exactly eighty-eight and fifty-five as adequate.

8
00:01:14.499 --> 00:01:19.664
For this rehab exam, use Hess's strict greater-than limits.

9
00:01:19.664 --> 00:01:36.386
If the patient has low resting oxygenation or desaturates significantly with exertion, give supplemental oxygen to maintain saturation; severe exercise hypoxemia may need an oxygen reservoir.

10
00:01:36.386 --> 00:01:45.667
If oxygenation still cannot be maintained, reduce intensity or stop exercise until it returns to adequate.

11
00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:51.807
The RCP 203 deck gives the step-by-step titration.

12
00:01:51.807 --> 00:02:00.814
First caution: avoid testing a patient with resting SpO2 under eighty-five percent on room air.

13
00:02:00.814 --> 00:02:12.854
During the test, if SpO2 stays at or above eighty-eight percent, the patient does not need additional oxygen and the test ends.

14
00:02:12.854 --> 00:02:27.929
If SpO2 drops by two percent or more, or falls below eighty-eight percent, increase oxygen by one liter per minute until SpO2 is back at or above eighty-eight.

15
00:02:27.929 --> 00:02:43.667
The same pair also qualifies COPD patients for long-term home oxygen, when SaO2 is at or below eighty-eight percent, or PaO2 at or below fifty-five torr, on room air.

16
00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:54.968
During an oxygen titration walk, your COPD patient's SpO2 falls from ninety three to eighty six percent.

17
00:02:54.968 --> 00:03:00.108
The patient's resting SpO2 on room air was ninety one.

18
00:03:00.108 --> 00:03:04.773
Should the test continue, and what do you adjust?

19
00:03:04.773 --> 00:03:06.392
Pause and answer.

20
00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:13.300
[6-second practice pause]

21
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:23.266
Testing was appropriate, because the resting SpO2 of ninety one is above the eighty five percent caution.

22
00:03:23.266 --> 00:03:33.633
During exercise the SpO2 fell below eighty eight and dropped by more than two percent, so the patient needs oxygen.

23
00:03:33.633 --> 00:03:38.682
So the test does not end here; you continue and titrate.

24
00:03:38.682 --> 00:03:46.525
Increase oxygen by one liter per minute and repeat until SpO2 is at least eighty eight.

25
00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:58.425
Hess adds that if adequate oxygenation still cannot be maintained, reduce the exercise intensity or stop until oxygenation recovers.

26
00:03:59.833 --> 00:04:10.782
Diaphragmatic breathing, or belly breathing, trains the patient to let the diaphragm do the work and rely less on the accessory muscles.

27
00:04:10.782 --> 00:04:22.053
The diaphragm contracts and moves downward, lungs expand more fully, and the abdomen pushes outward on inhalation and recoils on exhalation.

28
00:04:22.053 --> 00:04:28.332
The breaths are deeper and reach the lower lung, where gas exchange is better.

29
00:04:28.332 --> 00:04:36.463
It improves the efficiency of ventilation by increasing tidal volume and decreasing respiratory rate.

30
00:04:36.463 --> 00:04:43.386
It engages the parasympathetic nervous system, lowering heart rate and blood pressure.

31
00:04:43.386 --> 00:04:54.335
To teach it, place the patient in semi-Fowler's, forearms relaxed, knees bent, and put your hand on the upper abdomen below the xiphoid.

32
00:04:54.335 --> 00:05:05.283
The patient inhales slowly through the nose and pushes out against your hand, while you apply progressive resistance to end inspiration.

33
00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:12.088
Pursed-lip breathing is especially useful in COPD and emphysema.

34
00:05:12.088 --> 00:05:28.755
The patient inhales slowly through the nose for about two seconds, then exhales gently through lips held as if blowing out a candle for about four seconds, so exhalation is roughly twice inhalation.

35
00:05:28.755 --> 00:05:47.275
The gentle back-pressure splints small airways open, helping reduce dynamic airway collapse and air trapping, can reduce dyspnea, lets more CO2 leave, slows the respiratory rate, and gives the patient a sense of control.

36
00:05:47.275 --> 00:06:03.185
A deck conflict to know: the RCP 203 chapter ten deck says expiration should be at least two to three times inspiration, while the rehab deck uses about two seconds in and four seconds out.

37
00:06:03.185 --> 00:06:06.300
For this exam, follow the rehab deck.

38
00:06:07.700 --> 00:06:13.243
The deck's contrast to belly breathing is thoracic, or chest, breathing.

39
00:06:13.243 --> 00:06:24.867
In chest breathing the intercostals do most of the work, air enters the upper lung, the chest and shoulders rise, and the belly stays relatively still.

40
00:06:24.867 --> 00:06:30.025
It is shallow, less efficient, sympathetic, and linked with stress.

41
00:06:30.025 --> 00:06:36.107
Belly breathing is deep, efficient, parasympathetic, and engages the diaphragm.

42
00:06:36.107 --> 00:06:54.198
Do not confuse the deck's thoracic breathing with Hess's thoracic expansion exercises, deep breaths limited to three or four at a time to avoid fatigue and hyperventilation, used in the active cycle of breathing to mobilize secretions.

43
00:06:54.198 --> 00:06:59.125
Those are therapeutic deep breaths, not shallow chest breathing.

44
00:07:00.533 --> 00:07:02.122
Two cousins to know.

45
00:07:02.122 --> 00:07:17.934
Lateral costal breathing is an inspiratory exercise like diaphragmatic breathing, but you cup both hands over the lower rib edges and the patient breathes outward against your hands around the waist.

46
00:07:17.934 --> 00:07:25.006
It is a good alternative to the diaphragmatic method, especially after abdominal surgery.

47
00:07:25.006 --> 00:07:41.771
Accessory-muscle breathing uses the scalene, sternocleidomastoid, pectoralis major, trapezius, and external intercostals; they play a minor role in quiet breathing and become visible during exercise or distress.

48
00:07:41.771 --> 00:07:48.286
Prominent accessory use implies increased work of breathing or diaphragm weakness.

49
00:07:48.286 --> 00:08:02.508
After spinal cord injury, loss of intercostals and accessory muscles leaves isolated diaphragmatic breathing: rapid, shallow, with the chest wall moving inward during inspiration.

50
00:08:03.933 --> 00:08:05.221
Pause and think.

51
00:08:05.221 --> 00:08:09.325
A patient is breathing twenty-eight times a minute.

52
00:08:09.325 --> 00:08:16.729
Can you tell from the rate alone whether the patient is hyperventilating or hypoventilating?

53
00:08:16.729 --> 00:08:23.408
What single lab value defines the difference, and what are the two numeric cutoffs?

54
00:08:24.333 --> 00:08:30.333
[6-second practice pause]

55
00:08:30.833 --> 00:08:33.531
Rate alone cannot tell you.

56
00:08:33.531 --> 00:08:42.925
Hyperventilation and hypoventilation are defined by the PaCO2, not by how the breathing looks.

57
00:08:42.925 --> 00:08:47.821
PaCO2 varies inversely with alveolar ventilation.

58
00:08:47.821 --> 00:08:52.018
Normal PaCO2 is thirty-five to forty-five.

59
00:08:52.018 --> 00:09:09.905
Acute hyperventilation means PaCO2 below thirty-five with a pH above seven point four-five, acute respiratory alkalosis, most often caused by hypoxemia when PaO2 drops near sixty.

60
00:09:09.905 --> 00:09:33.888
Acute hypoventilation means PaCO2 above forty-five with pH below seven point three-five, acute respiratory acidosis, caused by narcotics, sedation, severe asthma or COPD exacerbations, airway obstruction, neuromuscular weakness, or fatigue.

61
00:09:33.888 --> 00:09:40.283
The trap: rapid, shallow breathing can still be hypoventilation.

62
00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.717
The study guide puts these five side by side.

63
00:09:45.717 --> 00:09:53.572
Diaphragmatic is the taught belly-breathing technique: deep, efficient, parasympathetic.

64
00:09:53.572 --> 00:10:01.160
Pursed-lip is the taught exhalation technique: back-pressure splints airways in COPD.

65
00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:08.212
Thoracic breathing is chest breathing, the deck's shallow sympathetic contrast.

66
00:10:08.212 --> 00:10:17.763
Hyperventilation and hypoventilation are not defined by how breathing looks; they are defined by the PaCO2.

67
00:10:17.763 --> 00:10:27.672
PaCO2 below thirty-five raises the pH, respiratory alkalosis; above forty-five lowers it, respiratory acidosis.

68
00:10:27.672 --> 00:10:34.009
Keep the trained techniques separate from the gas-exchange definitions.

69
00:10:34.009 --> 00:10:38.383
Diaphragmatic and pursed-lip are things we teach.

70
00:10:38.383 --> 00:10:43.650
Hyperventilation and hypoventilation are things we measure.

71
00:10:45.067 --> 00:10:55.504
Pulmonary rehab does not significantly improve PFTs; the FEV1 may stay essentially the same, but the patient walks farther.

72
00:10:55.504 --> 00:11:01.698
Training blends stretching and flexibility, strengthening, and endurance.

73
00:11:01.698 --> 00:11:11.286
Start the initial load at an intensity the patient can tolerate without undue discomfort and build progressively.

74
00:11:11.286 --> 00:11:13.153
Hess adds the numbers.

75
00:11:13.153 --> 00:11:22.656
Push toward a training effect, as much as seventy to eighty percent of predicted maximal heart rate if possible.

76
00:11:22.656 --> 00:11:29.444
Minimum endurance exercise is no less than twenty minutes, three times per week.

77
00:11:29.444 --> 00:11:43.106
After a bicycle graded exercise test, initial load is fifty to eighty percent of maximum watts for the bicycle and thirty to forty percent for the arm ergometer.

78
00:11:43.106 --> 00:11:51.167
The six-minute walk is the method of choice to assess exercise capacity before and after rehab.

79
00:11:52.567 --> 00:12:00.676
The Modified Borg Scale rates perceived exertion or dyspnea from zero, nothing at all, to ten, maximal.

80
00:12:00.676 --> 00:12:06.187
A Borg effort between four and six means the prescription is adequate.

81
00:12:06.187 --> 00:12:19.100
Advance the load when the patient finishes the session with Borg under four, heart rate under eighty percent of predicted maximum, and acceptable oxygen saturation.

82
00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:25.556
Increase the load for three to five minutes, then return to the previous workload.

83
00:12:25.556 --> 00:12:32.642
Training benefits are specific to the muscles and tasks trained, so walking is emphasized.

84
00:12:34.067 --> 00:12:46.772
Keep oxygenation above eighty-eight percent SpO2 and fifty-five millimeters PaO2 during exercise; add oxygen, reduce intensity, or stop if you cannot.

85
00:12:46.772 --> 00:13:01.934
Teach diaphragmatic breathing to engage the diaphragm and relax accessory muscles, and pursed-lip breathing about two seconds in and four seconds out to splint small airways open.

86
00:13:01.934 --> 00:13:11.252
Thoracic chest breathing is the shallow contrast pattern, and is not the same as thoracic expansion exercises.

87
00:13:11.252 --> 00:13:17.266
Hyperventilation and hypoventilation are defined by PaCO2, not by rate.

88
00:13:17.266 --> 00:13:31.242
Push toward seventy to eighty percent of predicted max heart rate if possible, at least twenty minutes three times a week, and use the Modified Borg to advance load.
